## CDC & SCD Type 2 — Batch and Streaming Implementation

---

### 1. Core Concepts

| Term | What It Means | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **CDC (Change Data Capture)** | Capturing row-level changes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) from a source system | Database binlog, Kafka events, Delta CDF |
| **SCD Type 1** | Overwrite the old value — no history kept | Customer address updated → old address gone |
| **SCD Type 2** | Keep full history with effective dates — old row marked closed, new row inserted | Customer address changed → old row gets `end_date = today`, new row gets `start_date = today` |
| **SCD Type 3** | Add columns for old/new values (rarely used) | `current_address` + `previous_address` columns |
| **Change Data Feed (CDF)** | Delta Lake feature that records changes as they happen to a Delta table | `SELECT * FROM table_changes('my_table', 2)` |
| **Sequence key** | Column that orders events chronologically | Timestamp, version number, Kafka offset |

### 2. SCD Type 1 vs Type 2 Decision

| Question | Type 1 (Overwrite) | Type 2 (History) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Do you need historical values? | No | Yes |
| "What was the customer's address last January?" | Cannot answer | Can answer |
| Storage cost | Lower (1 row per entity) | Higher (N rows per entity over time) |
| Query complexity | Simple | Needs `WHERE is_current = true` or date range filter |
| MERGE pattern | `WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE` | Close old row + INSERT new row (2 operations) |
| Use case | Reference/dimension data where only latest matters | Audit, compliance, temporal reporting |

---

### 3. SCD Type 1 — Batch (MERGE)

The simplest CDC pattern. Source provides the latest state; you upsert into target.

```sql
-- SCD Type 1: Overwrite existing, insert new
MERGE INTO gold.customers AS target
USING staging.customers_daily AS source
  ON target.customer_id = source.customer_id
WHEN MATCHED AND (
  target.name != source.name OR
  target.email != source.email OR
  target.address != source.address
) THEN UPDATE SET
  target.name = source.name,
  target.email = source.email,
  target.address = source.address,
  target.updated_at = current_timestamp()
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (
  customer_id, name, email, address, updated_at
) VALUES (
  source.customer_id, source.name, source.email, source.address, current_timestamp()
);
```

**Key points:**
- One row per customer in target (always latest)
- The `AND (target.name != source.name OR ...)` avoids unnecessary updates when nothing changed
- Idempotent — running twice with same data has no effect

